# tests/backrefs/d27/gating.rxt -- D27 blinded acceptance corpus, module
# `backrefs`, BOTH refusal directions of the module gate. Unlike every
# other file in this corpus, every cell here HAS been run against real
# `build/pcrec` (the brief's explicitly permitted exception: "you may run
# it only to confirm refusal-direction cells"), because these are
# assertions about pcrec's CURRENT refusal behavior, not about future
# match semantics. All `perr` blocks; `perr` only asserts nonzero exit,
# never diagnostic wording (docs/testing.md), so no cell here depends on
# pcrec's exact message text -- only on the fact that it refuses.
#
# TWO REFUSAL DIRECTIONS, confirmed distinct today (different messages,
# same nonzero exit -- the `perr` block can't see the difference, but the
# distinction is real and worth a comment trail for whoever reads this at
# merge):
#   1. MODULE DISABLED: the feature list passed to pcrec does not include
#      `backrefs` at all -> "requires module 'backrefs'".
#   2. MODULE ENABLED, NOT YET IMPLEMENTED: `backrefs` IS in the feature
#      list, but the module is unbuilt -> "module 'backrefs' is enabled
#      but ... is not implemented yet".
#
# A FINDING WORTH FLAGGING AT MERGE (recorded in full in README.md): the
# DUPNAMES/(?J) construct does not follow this two-state pattern at all.
# `(?J)` itself is gated by module `modifiers` (not `backrefs`), and the
# actual duplicate-name RESOLUTION logic is attributed to module
# `named-groups` (not `backrefs`), with a message reading "does not
# implement duplicate group names" / "out of pcrec's scope" -- wording
# that reads as more final than the "not implemented yet" wording every
# other backrefs construct gets. Cells 9-11 below document today's actual
# behavior; none of them assert what SHOULD eventually be true, since
# that is a module-ownership question for the merge review, not this
# lane's to settle.

# --- 1. Numeric backreference, module fully disabled. ---
pattern (a)\1
features classes
perr

# --- 2. Numeric backreference, module enabled but unimplemented. ---
pattern (a)\1
features backrefs
perr

# --- 3. Octal/backref escape \8, module disabled -- confirms the gate
# applies to the ESCAPE RECOGNITION itself, before the octal-vs-backref
# disambiguation (F4) is ever reached. ---
pattern \8
features classes
perr

# --- 4. \g (any bracket form), module disabled. ---
pattern (a|b)\g<1>
features classes
perr

# --- 5. \g, module enabled but unimplemented (including the
# subroutine-spelled forms -- pcrec's recognition gate covers \g
# uniformly regardless of which bracket follows it; see spellings.rxt's
# out-of-scope note for why match SEMANTICS of \g<name>/\g'name' are not
# tested by this corpus). ---
pattern (a|b)\g<1>
features backrefs
perr

# --- 6. \k, module disabled. ---
pattern (?<n>a)\k<n>
features classes,named-groups
perr

# --- 7. \k, module enabled (plus named-groups, which IS built) but
# backrefs itself is unimplemented. ---
pattern (?<n>a)\k<n>
features backrefs,named-groups
perr

# --- 8. (?P=n), module disabled. ---
pattern (?P<n>a)(?P=n)
features classes,named-groups
perr

# --- 9. (?J), with modifiers present, and NO duplicate name anywhere in
# the pattern at all. MEASURED, not assumed: pcrec refuses this too, and
# with the SAME "module 'named-groups' does not implement duplicate
# group names" message as the genuine-duplicate cell below -- pcrec
# refuses the (?J) directive itself, unconditionally, once its syntax is
# recognized, rather than deferring to whether a collision is ever
# actually declared. Worth knowing before assuming cell 10 below is
# testing something different from this one; today, it is not. ---
pattern (?J)a
features modifiers
perr

# --- 10. (?J) with a genuine duplicate name, modifiers AND named-groups
# both present (today's finding: the refusal names module `named-groups`,
# not `backrefs`, and the wording says the spelling is "out of pcrec's
# scope" rather than "not implemented yet"). ---
pattern (?J)(?<a>x)(?<a>y)
features modifiers,named-groups,backrefs
perr

# --- 11. (?J) with modifiers EXCLUDED (an explicit, non-default feature
# list that leaves modifiers out) refuses on the (?J) SYNTAX itself,
# before dupname resolution is ever reached -- a different refusal point
# than cell 10's, both perr, and the .rxt format can't see the
# difference in wording, but the comment trail is worth keeping since
# both are genuinely different refusal sites (module `modifiers`'s own
# gate vs. module `named-groups`'s dupname refusal). ---
pattern (?J)(?<a>x)(?<a>y)
features backrefs,named-groups
perr
